<title>USA: CME to launch &quot;mini&quot; S&amp;P 500 derivatives.</title>
<headline>CME to launch &quot;mini&quot; S&amp;P 500 derivatives.</headline>
<dateline>CHICAGO 1997-06-05</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Chicago Mercantile Exchange said Thursday it planned to introduce a &quot;mini&quot; version of its flagship S&amp;P 500 stock index futures and futures options.</p>
<p>The CME said in a statement the multiplier for the new contracts will be $50 times the index value, one-tenth the size of the current contracts.  </p>
<p>The CME said the new contracts are subject to approval from exchange board and regulators.</p>
<p>The exchange said it is also continuing its discussions regarding the current size of the S&amp;P 500 index contracts and that a decision on whether to split the size of the contracts will be forthcoming.</p>
<p>Thursday morning, June S&amp;P 500 futures were up 4.50 at 846.60.</p>
<p>((--Derivatives desk, 312 408 8750))</p>
